<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lesbos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lesbos is a large Greek island in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, close to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with its chief town at Mytilene. Probably the Lazpa of Hittite texts, it was home in the archaic period to the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> and to the rival cities of Mytilene and Methymna. From 1355 it was ruled by the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gattilusio">Gattilusio family</a> until the Ottoman conquest of 1462, and it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lesbos.jpg" alt="Lesbos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koutrolm · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mytilene_2010-04-03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
